 for Set A of the Class XII Mathematics Half-Yearly Examination 2026-27.\\n\\n## FILE 1: Set A Question Paper\\nPath: C:\\\\Users\\\\rajan\\\\.gemini\\\\antigravity\\\\scratch\\\\math_exam_2026\\\\Set_A_Question_Paper.txt\\n\\nFormat it EXACTLY like a CBSE exam paper. Include:\\n- Header: HALF-YEARLY EXAMINATION 2026-27, CLASS XII - MATHEMATICS (SET A)\\n- Time Allowed: 3 Hours, Maximum Marks: 80\\n- General Instructions (9 points as per CBSE pattern)\\n- All 5 sections\\n\\nHere are ALL the questions:\\n\\n--- SECTION A (20 x 1 = 20 marks) ---\\n\\nQ1. Let A = {1, 2, 3} and R = {(1,1), (2,2), (3,3), (1,2)} be a relation on A. Then R is:\\n(a) An equivalence relation    (b) Reflexive and transitive only\\n(c) Reflexive and symmetric only    (d) Symmetric and transitive only\\n\\nQ2. The function f : R -> R defined by f(x) = x² is:\\n(a) One-one and onto    (b) One-one but not onto\\n(c) Onto but not one-one    (d) Neither one-one nor onto\\n\\nQ3. The principal value of sin⁻¹(-1/2) is:\\n(a) π/6    (b) -π/6    (c) 5π/6    (d) -5π/6\\n\\nQ4. The domain of cos⁻¹(2x - 1) is:\\n(a) [0, 1]    (b) [-1, 1]    (c) [-1, 0]    (d) [0, 2]\\n\\nQ5. For any square matrix A, the matrix (A + Aᵀ) is always:\\n(a) Skew-symmetric    (b) Symmetric    (c) A diagonal matrix    (d) A null matrix\\n\\nQ6. The value of x for which the matrix\\n        | 0   1  -2 |\\nA =     |-1   0   x |\\n        | 2  -3   0 |\\nis skew-symmetric, is:\\n(a) 3    (b) -3    (c) 2    (d) -2\\n\\nQ7. If A = [[1, 2], [3, 4]], then (A - Aᵀ) is always:\\n(a) A symmetric matrix    (b) A skew-symmetric matrix\\n(c) A null matrix    (d) An identity matrix\\n\\nQ8. If A is a 3 x 3 matrix such that |A| = 5, then the value of |3A| is:\\n(a) 15    (b) 45    (c) 135    (d) 405\\n\\nQ9. If A is a square matrix of order 3 with |A| = 4, then |adj A| equals:\\n(a) 4    (b) 8    (c) 16    (d) 64\\n\\nQ10. The function f(x) = |x| is:\\n(a) Continuous and differentiable everywhere\\n(b) Continuous everywhere but not differentiable at x = 0\\n(c) Discontinuous at x = 0\\n(d) Differentiable everywhere but not continuous\\n\\nQ11. If y = e²ˣ, then dy/dx equals:\\n(a) e²ˣ    (b) 2e²ˣ    (c) 2xe²ˣ    (d) eˣ\\n\\nQ12. If f(x) = kx + 1 for x ≤ 5 and f(x) = 3x - 5 for x > 5 is continuous at x = 5, then k =:\\n(a) 9/5    (b) 5/9    (c) 2    (d) 3\\n\\nQ13. The rate of change of the area of a circle with respect to its radius r, at r = 5 cm, is:\\n(a) 5π cm²/cm    (b) 10π cm²/cm    (c) 25π cm²/cm    (d) 2π cm²/cm\\n\\nQ14. The function f(x) = x² - 4x + 6 is strictly increasing in the interval:\\n(a) (-∞, 2)    (b) (2, ∞)    (c) (-2, 2)    (d) R\\n\\nQ15. The point on the curve y = x² at which the tangent is parallel to the x-axis is:\\n(a) (1, 1)    (b) (0, 0)    (c) (2, 4)    (d) (-1, 1)\\n\\nQ16. The slope of the tangent to the curve y = x³ - 3x + 2 at x = 1 is:\\n(a) 0    (b) 3    (c) -3    (d) 1\\n\\nQ17. ∫ sec²x dx equals:\\n(a) tan x + C    (b) -cot x + C    (c) sec x tan x + C    (d) cot x + C\\n\\nQ18. ∫ 1/(1 + x²) dx equals:\\n(a) tan⁻¹x + C    (b) sin⁻¹x + C    (c) cot⁻¹x + C    (d) sec⁻¹x + C\\n\\nQ19. Assertion-Reason:\\nAssertion (A): The function f(x) = |x - 2| is continuous at x = 2.\\nReason (R): The function f(x) = |x - 2| is differentiable at x = 2.\\n(a) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.\\n(b) Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.\\n(c) A is true, but R is false.\\n(d) A is false, but R is true.\\n\\nQ20. Assertion-Reason:\\nAssertion (A): The point (3, 4) marked on the graph of f(x) = -(x - 3)² + 4 is a point of local maximum.\\nReason (R): At a point of local maximum, f'(x) = 0 and f''(x) < 0.\\n(a) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.\\n(b) Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.\\n(c) A is true, but R is false.\\n(d) A is false, but R is true.\\n\\n--- SECTION B (5 x 2 = 10 marks) ---\\n\\nQ21. Check whether the relation R = {(1,1), (2,2), (3,3), (1,2), (2,3)} on the set A = {1, 2, 3} is reflexive, symmetric and transitive. Justify your answer.\\n\\nQ22. If A = [[1, 2], [3, 4]] and B = [[2, 0], [1, 3]], find 2A - 3B.\\n\\nQ23. Differentiate y = sin(x² + 1) with respect to x.\\n\\nQ24. Find the slope of the tangent to the curve y = x³ - x at x = 2.\\n\\nQ25. Evaluate: ∫ x·e^(x²) dx.\\n\\n--- SECTION C (6 x 3 = 18 marks) ---\\n\\nQ26. Find the value of:\\n(i) sin⁻¹(sin(2π/3))    (ii) cos⁻¹(cos(7π/6))    (iii) tan⁻¹(tan(3π/4))\\n\\nQ27. Find the value of: tan⁻¹(1) + cos⁻¹(-1/2) + sin⁻¹(-1/2).\\n\\nQ28. Using minors and cofactors, evaluate the determinant:\\n| 1  2  3 |\\n| 0  1  4 |\\n| 5  6  0 |\\n\\nQ29. If y = xˣ (x > 0), find dy/dx.\\n\\nQ30. Find the intervals in which the function f(x) = 2x³ - 15x² + 36x + 1 is (i) strictly increasing (ii) strictly decreasing.\\n\\nQ31. Evaluate ∫ x·sin x dx using integration by parts.\\n\\n--- SECTION D (4 x 5 = 20 marks) ---\\n\\nQ32. Express the matrix A = [[1, 2, -3], [3, -1, 2], [-2, 1, 4]] as the sum of a symmetric and a skew-symmetric matrix.\\n\\nQ33. Using the matrix method, solve the following system of equations:\\nx + y + z = 6\\ny + 3z = 11\\nx - 2y + z = 0\\n\\nQ34. If y = (sin⁻¹x)², prove that (1 - x²) d²y/dx² - x dy/dx - 2 = 0.\\n\\nQ35. Evaluate: ∫ (x² + 1)/(x² - 5x + 6) dx.\\n\\n--- SECTION E (3 x 4 = 12 marks) ---\\nEach case-based question has sub-parts of 2, 1 and 1 marks.\\n\\nQ36. Case Study - Relations and Functions\\nTwo friends, Rohan and Simran, are studying relations defined on sets. Consider the set A = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5} and the relation R on A defined by R = {(a, b) : |a - b| is even}.\\n(i) Show that R is an equivalence relation on A.  (2 marks)\\n(ii) Write the equivalence class of the element 2.  (1 mark)\\n(iii) Is (1, 5) ∈ R? Justify your answer.  (1 mark)\\n\\nQ37. Case Study - Application of Derivatives\\nA company's total cost of producing x units of a product is given by C(x) = 0.005x³ - 0.02x² + 30x + 5000 (in ₹).\\n(i) Find the marginal cost function, MC(x) = dC/dx.  (2 marks)\\n(ii) Find the marginal cost when x = 3 units.  (1 mark)\\n(iii) State, with reason, whether the cost is increasing or decreasing at x = 3.  (1 mark)\\n\\nQ38. Case Study - Integrals\\nA particle moves along a straight line such that its velocity at time t seconds is v(t) = 3t² - 12t + 9 (in m/s). It is given that the particle starts at the origin, i.e. s(0) = 0.\\n(i) Find the displacement function s(t).  (2 marks)\\n(ii) Find the displacement of the particle at t = 2 s.  (1 mark)\\n(iii) Find the value(s) of t ∈ [0, 3] at which the particle is momentarily at rest.  (1 mark)\\n\\nEnd with: -- End of Question Paper --\\n\\n## FILE 2: Set A Marking Scheme\\nPath: C:\\\\Users\\\\rajan\\\\.gemini\\\\antigravity\\\\scratch\\\\math_exam_2026\\\\Set_A_Marking_Scheme.txt\\n\\nFormat: For each question, show:\\n- Question number\\n- Complete step-by-step solution\\n- Mark allocation for each step (e.g., [1 mark], [½ mark])\\n- Final answer clearly boxed or highlighted\\n\\nHere are ALL the answers:\\n\\nQ1: (b) Reflexive and transitive only\\nQ2: (d) Neither one-one nor onto\\nQ3: (b) -π/6\\nQ4: (a) [0, 1]\\nQ5: (b) Symmetric\\nQ6: (a) 3\\nQ7: (b) A skew-symmetric matrix\\nQ8: (c) 135\\nQ9: (c) 16\\nQ10: (b) Continuous everywhere but not differentiable at x = 0\\nQ11: (b) 2e²ˣ\\nQ12: (a) 9/5\\nQ13: (b) 10π cm²/cm\\nQ14: (b) (2, ∞)\\nQ15: (b) (0, 0)\\nQ16: (a) 0\\nQ17: (a) tan x + C\\nQ18: (a) tan⁻¹x + C\\nQ19: (c) A is true, but R is false\\nQ20: (a) Both A and R are true, R is the correct explanation\\n\\nFor Section A MCQs: Show correct option AND brief justification (1 mark each).\\n\\nQ21 (2 marks): Reflexive: (1,1),(2,2),(3,3) ∈ R ✓ [½]. Symmetric: (1,2) ∈ R but (2,1) ∉ R, NOT symmetric [½]. Transitive: (1,2),(2,3) ∈ R but (1,3) ∉ R, NOT transitive [1].\\n\\nQ22 (2 marks): 2A = [[2,4],[6,8]], 3B = [[6,0],[3,9]] [1]. 2A - 3B = [[-4,4],[3,-1]] [1].\\n\\nQ23 (2 marks): dy/dx = cos(x²+1) · d/dx(x²+1) [1] = 2x cos(x²+1) [1].\\n\\nQ24 (2 marks): dy/dx = 3x² - 1 [1]. At x=2: slope = 3(4)-1 = 11 [1].\\n\\nQ25 (2 marks): Let u = x², du = 2x dx [1]. ∫ = (1/2)e^u + C = (1/2)e^(x²) + C [1].\\n\\nQ26 (3 marks):\\n(i) sin(2π/3) = sin(π - π/3) = sin(π/3) = √3/2. sin⁻¹(√3/2) = π/3. Since 2π/3 ∉ [-π/2, π/2], sin⁻¹(sin(2π/3)) = π - 2π/3 = π/3 [1]\\n(ii) cos(7π/6) = cos(π + π/6) = -cos(π/6) = -√3/2. cos⁻¹(-√3/2) = π - π/6 = 5π/6 [1]\\n(iii) tan(3π/4) = tan(π - π/4) = -tan(π/4) = -1. tan⁻¹(-1) = -π/4 [1]\\n\\nQ27 (3 marks): tan⁻¹(1) = π/4 [1]. cos⁻¹(-1/2) = 2π/3 [½]. sin⁻¹(-1/2) = -π/6 [½]. Sum = π/4 + 2π/3 - π/6 = 3π/12 + 8π/12 - 2π/12 = 9π/12 = 3π/4 [1].\\n\\nQ28 (3 marks): Expand along R1:\\n= 1(0-24) - 2(0-20) + 3(0-5) [1 for cofactors]\\n= -24 + 40 - 15 [1 for computation]\\n= 1 [1 for answer]\\n\\nQ29 (3 marks): Take log: ln y = x ln x [½]. Differentiate: (1/y)(dy/dx) = 1 + ln x [1½]. dy/dx = xˣ(1 + ln x) [1].\\n\\nQ30 (3 marks): f'(x) = 6x² - 30x + 36 = 6(x-2)(x-3) [1]. Sign analysis: f'(x) > 0 for x < 2 or x > 3 [½]. f'(x) < 0 for 2 < x < 3 [½]. (i) Increasing on (-∞, 2) ∪ (3, ∞) [½]. (ii) Decreasing on (2, 3) [½].\\n\\nQ31 (3 marks): By parts: u = x, dv = sin x dx [½]. du = dx, v = -cos x [½]. ∫ x sin x dx = -x cos x + ∫ cos x dx [1]. = -x cos x + sin x + C [1].\\n\\nQ32 (5 marks):\\nAᵀ = [[1,3,-2],[2,-1,1],[-3,2,4]] [1]\\nP = (A+Aᵀ)/2 = [[1, 5/2, -5/2], [5/2, -1, 3/2], [-5/2, 3/2, 4]] [2]\\nQ = (A-Aᵀ)/2 = [[0, -1/2, -1/2], [1/2, 0, 1/2], [1/2, -1/2, 0]] [1]\\nVerify: P is symmetric, Q is skew-symmetric, P + Q = A [1]\\n\\nQ33 (5 marks):\\nA = [[1,1,1],[0,1,3],[1,-2,1]], |A| = 1(1+6)-1(0-3)+1(0-1) = 7+3-1 = 9 [1]\\nCofactors and adj(A) = [[7,-3,2],[3,0,-3],[-1,3,1]] [2]\\nA⁻¹ = (1/9) adj(A) [½]\\nX = A⁻¹B = (1/9)[[7,-3,2],[3,0,-3],[-1,3,1]][[6],[11],[0]] = [[1],[2],[3]] [1½]\\nSo x = 1, y = 2, z = 3.\\n\\nQ34 (5 marks):\\ny₁ = 2sin⁻¹x/√(1-x²) [1]\\n√(1-x²)·y₁ = 2sin⁻¹x ... (*) [1]\\nDifferentiate (*): √(1-x²)·y₂ + y₁·(-x/√(1-x²)) = 2/√(1-x²) [1½]\\nMultiply by √(1-x²): (1-x²)y₂ - xy₁ = 2 [1]\\nHence (1-x²)y₂ - xy₁ - 